                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static memory area. If this memory is modified, it can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. If you must use `std::getenv`, ensure that the returned pointer is not directly modified and that the returned string is copied to a buffer that is large enough to hold the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::getenv`:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (buffer_size != nullptr) {\n    // Copy the string to a local buffer to avoid modifying the original string\n    std::string buffer_size_str(buffer_size);\n    // Use the copied string\n    bytes_Work = size_Work;\n} else {\n    bytes_Work = sizeof(T) * size_Work;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `std::getenv`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
