              "id": "e3186482-d33a-386c-994d-a7bee2c90213",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that represents the value for the specified environment variable. The problem is that the returned string can be modified by subsequent calls to `std::getenv`, `std::setenv`, or `std::unsetenv`, and this can lead to race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` in multithreaded applications. If you need to use environment variables, consider copying the values into your own buffers to avoid race conditions. Also, always check the returned pointer from `std::getenv` for null before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (buffer_size != nullptr) {\n    sz = lwork;\n} else {\n    sz = sizeof(rocblas_double_complex) * lwork;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `std::getenv`\n- `rocblas` for `rocblas_double_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
