              "id": "35dc51de-0790-3a44-8f7e-9de2dffa0818",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static buffer, which can be overwritten by subsequent calls to `getenv` or `setenv`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` and instead use safer alternatives that do not have these issues. For example, consider using a function that copies the environment variable into a buffer that you control, or use a higher-level interface that abstracts away these details.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::getenv`:\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer = new char[256];\nstd::strcpy(buffer, std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\"));\nsz = buffer != nullptr ? lwork : sizeof(double) * lwork;\ndelete[] buffer;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n- `<cstring>` for `std::strcpy`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
